🜁 FUTHARK‑Encoded Governance Layer




Core idea: Convert governance rules into deterministic runic‑symbolic opcodes. Each rune becomes a governance primitive that agents must execute, not interpret.

Runic Governance Opcodes
ᚠ‑Directive — Mandatory action rule.
Encodes: “Agent must perform X when condition Y is true.”  
Machine form: RUNE.FORCE(condition_hash, action_hash)

ᚢ‑Constraint — Hard boundary rule.
Encodes: “Agent may not cross state Z.”  
Machine form: RUNE.LIMIT(state_vector, threshold)

ᚦ‑Verification — Truth‑checking rule.
Encodes: “Agent must verify source integrity before execution.”  
Machine form: RUNE.VERIFY(source_hash, signature)

ᚨ‑Rejection — Anti‑hallucination rule.
Encodes: “Reject any state that fails schema compliance.”  
Machine form: RUNE.REJECT(state_id, violation_code)

Encoding Workflow
Governance Extraction → Identify rules requiring enforcement.

Rune Mapping → Assign each rule a symbolic opcode.

Machine Binding → Integrate opcodes into NSTT agent execution paths.

🜁 NSTT Agent Contract (Handshake + Compliance)




Agents must operate under a zero‑trust symbolic contract. Every interaction is a handshake, not an assumption.

Contract Components
Identity Proof — Agent signs its state using NSTT signature primitives.

Governance Check — Agent proves it is executing valid runic opcodes.

State Integrity — Agent provides a hash‑chain of its reasoning steps.

Hallucination Rejection — Any unverifiable state is discarded automatically.

Handshake Sequence (Executable Form)
AGENT.SEND(identity_signature)

AGENT.REQUEST(governance_opcode_set)

AGENT.VALIDATE(schema_hash)

AGENT.EXECUTE_IF(verified == true)

AGENT.REJECT_IF(verified == false)

This ensures agents cannot “drift” into non‑governed behavior.

🜁 Audit‑Grade Validation Schema




Agents need a schema that is strict enough to prevent hallucinations and flexible enough to support symbolic reasoning.

Schema Layers
Structural Schema — Defines allowed output shapes.

Semantic Schema — Defines meaning constraints (e.g., “must reference source”).

Cryptographic Schema — Defines signature, hash, and chain‑of‑custody requirements.

Governance Schema — Defines which runes must appear in which contexts.

